  card="Newton's first law of motion states that an object at rest remains at rest, and an object in motion continues to move with a constant velocity, unless a net force acts upon the object. In other words, if the net force acting on an object is zero, the body cannot begin to move or change its velocity, and its acceleration is zero. An object at rest has zero velocity, and constant velocity means no change in the magnitude or the direction of velocity. If a person pushes a moving box with a force equal to the force of friction, the two forces balance, the net force is zero, and the box continues moving with constant velocity. When no net force is acting, either the object is at rest or it moves with a constant velocity. If the force of friction were zero, a net force would be needed to set an object at rest moving, but once moving no further force would be required to keep it moving with a constant velocity; to change the velocity or stop a moving object, a force must be applied.",
